Add an abortable wait and loop back while the rule’s condition remains true. This complete small native rule set gives each player a counter, increments immediately when Interact is pressed, and then repeats with a requested 0.1-second delay while held. Releasing stops it; pressing again resumes counting.

```workshop
variables
{
    player:
        0: Ticks
}

rule("Initialize each player's counter")
{
    event
    {
        Ongoing - Each Player;
        All;
        All;
    }
    actions
    {
        Event Player.Ticks = 0;
    }
}

rule("Count while Interact is held")
{
    event
    {
        Ongoing - Each Player;
        All;
        All;
    }
    conditions
    {
        Is Button Held(Event Player, Button(Interact)) == True;
    }
    actions
    {
        Modify Player Variable(Event Player, Ticks, Add, 1);
        Wait(0.1, Abort When False);
        Loop If Condition Is True;
    }
}
```

`Ongoing - Each Player` is edge-triggered: the actions start when all conditions become true. Keeping Interact held leaves the condition true, so your original one-action rule finishes and does not trigger again until release and another press. `Loop If Condition Is True` explicitly repeats the actions; `Wait` paces those repetitions. `Abort When False` ends the running action list when you release during the wait.

Timing caveat: the bundled documentation’s 2026-09-29 snapshot reports ordinary-map waits rounded up to 16 ms multiples, so `Wait(0.1, ...)` takes approximately 0.112 seconds rather than an exact 0.100 seconds. Check cadence on your intended map; Practice Range is documented as having different timing.

Validation: checked the structure, declarations, action signatures, and control flow against the bundled native fixtures and API references. This new example is uncompiled and has not been imported or game-tested. Paste it into the Workshop rule editor of a custom game, then watch `Ticks` in the Inspector while holding, releasing, and pressing again; another player should have an independent counter.
